"""Additional original explanations, examples and learner checklists."""
COURSE_EXTRA = {
'noorani-qaida': [
('Noorani Qaida for kids and adults: choosing the first exercise', '''The first useful exercise depends on what the learner can recognise without help. A child who can recite letter names may still need practice connecting a printed letter with its sound. An adult may recognise several shapes but be unfamiliar with the way a letter changes when joined. Explain those abilities separately during the trial. Ask the teacher to demonstrate a small example, hear an attempt and explain the next step. This is more informative than selecting a starting page solely from age or the name of a previous book.'''),
('Arabic letters, vowels and connected words', '''A simple practice sequence can move from recognising a shape to reading it with a short vowel and then encountering it in a suitable joined example. The tutor should choose the order and difficulty. If the learner relies on the remembered order of a page, ask how recognition will be checked when the examples change. When two forms or sounds are confused, slow the task down and identify the distinction before adding several new rules. A secure small step gives the next lesson something reliable to build on.'''),
('When to move from Qaida to Quran reading', '''Finishing a book does not by itself establish readiness for independent reading. Ask the teacher to check an appropriate unfamiliar exercise, including recognition, joining and the signs already taught. The learner may be ready to begin supported Quran reading while continuing selected foundation practice. That decision should come from the assessment, with a clear explanation of what remains difficult. If the next text causes repeated guessing, discuss the underlying skill rather than assuming that more pages or a faster reading pace will resolve the problem.''')],
'quran-reading-tajweed': [
('How live Quran recitation correction works', '''A useful correction has several parts: the teacher identifies an issue, demonstrates the intended recitation, listens to another attempt and checks the result. Ask what changed and how you can recognise it. If several errors occur together, the tutor may choose a manageable priority rather than interrupting every word with multiple technical terms. Bring uncertainty back to the lesson instead of rehearsing an approximation many times. A clear correction process is especially valuable when the learner cannot yet reliably hear the distinction in their own recitation.'''),
('Tajweed practice for unfamiliar Quran passages', '''Familiar passages can make reading feel more secure because their sequence is already remembered. Ask how a correction will be applied to another suitable example. This checks whether the learner follows the written text and understands where the taught rule belongs. The new example should remain appropriate to the current level; difficulty in unrelated vocabulary or letter recognition can obscure the skill being checked. Fluency can develop gradually as accurate reading requires fewer prompts, rather than through an instruction to increase speed before the foundation is dependable.'''),
('Reviewing progress in online Tajweed lessons', '''At a review, identify one recurring issue and ask what evidence shows improvement. It might be a clearer sound, a more consistent application of a taught rule or less reliance on a prompt. Explain where the correction still feels uncertain. A useful next task should follow that observation, so practice is connected with actual reading rather than a general request to do more. Detailed advanced study can be considered later if the teacher finds the reading foundation suitable and can explain what the additional work would address.''')],
'quran-memorization-hifz': [
('Planning new Hifz alongside recent and older revision', '''New memorisation, recent review and older recall compete for the same finite practice time. Ask the tutor to describe the actual task in each part of the plan. Terms such as sabaq, sabqi and manzil can help organise a discussion, but the amount and method still need explanation. A learner should know what to prepare, how to flag uncertainty and which material the next lesson will check. Do not treat a large new target as a substitute for a defined system of revision.'''),
('What to do when memorised passages become uncertain', '''If a passage repeatedly weakens, explain where the hesitation or error occurs and whether it changes after prompting. The teacher can listen and decide whether the issue concerns initial accuracy, recall, similarity between passages or the amount of review. Avoid guessing at the cause from a page total alone. The practical response may involve a smaller new target, a more focused review or a clearer correction before further repetition. The adjustment should be discussed openly, rather than allowing a revision backlog to grow behind an apparently successful new-memorisation record.'''),
('Online Hifz classes for a sustainable family routine', '''Describe an ordinary school or working week before setting expectations from holiday availability. Consider where accurate independent practice can happen and what support is realistic. A child may need help organising materials without receiving uncertain corrections from a parent. An adult may need a private, consistent window around work. Review the arrangement when circumstances change and ask which part of the plan should remain stable. Memorisation is an individual learning process; a guide or advertised timetable cannot establish a dependable completion date for an unassessed learner.''')],
 'tafseer': [
('Online Tafseer lessons: reading, translation and explanation', '''Being able to recite a passage, reading a translation and studying its explanation are related but distinct activities. Describe your present reading and understanding so the teacher can explain the intended level. Ask what text or recognised reference will guide the discussion and how unfamiliar Arabic vocabulary will be handled. A useful class should make clear what has been explained and what requires further study, rather than treating a translated phrase as a complete account of context or every possible interpretive question.'''),
('Preparing questions for a guided Tafseer class', '''Bring a short passage or a question that arose in previous study. State whether the difficulty concerns a word, the connection between verses or a broader idea, and let the teacher establish the relevant context. Avoid turning a brief online explanation into a standalone quotation without its qualifications. If a question involves a personal religious ruling, ask whether it needs a suitably qualified scholar rather than assuming that a general course description or a short educational note can resolve the individual situation.'''),
('Keeping useful notes without confusing subjects', '''Organise notes so the verse reference, vocabulary, explanation and unresolved question remain distinguishable. A learner can then revisit what was actually covered and ask for clarification in the next session. If reading needs substantial support, discuss how to address it alongside understanding instead of trying to conceal that need. Tafseer uses the separate published fee plans shown on the pricing page; confirm the course arrangement and teaching language directly. The appropriate pace should allow comprehension and careful questions, not merely the completion of an impressive quantity of notes.''')],
'advanced-tajweed': [
('Advanced Tajweed requirements: a dependable reading foundation', '''Advanced study is most useful when the teacher can distinguish detailed recitation issues from underlying uncertainty in reading. Bring a passage that shows the difficulty you want to work on and ask what the assessment reveals. A learner may read familiar material quickly while still needing help with another appropriate passage. Revisiting that foundation is a practical learning decision, not evidence that earlier study had no value. The tutor should explain why a particular topic is appropriate before adding technical terminology or a demanding practice target.'''),
('Makharij and letter qualities in practical recitation', '''Technical descriptions should connect with something the learner can hear and attempt. Ask for a clear model of the relevant distinction and enough time to try again inside a suitable word or phrase. If the explanation remains abstract, describe the point of confusion rather than guessing. Detailed study should help the learner recognise and apply the correction in recitation, with the teacher checking the result. Naming an articulation point or rule accurately does not alone establish that the skill is secure when reading independently.'''),
('Checking consistency beyond a prepared passage', '''After working on a detail, ask the tutor to select another suitable example and explain what is being checked. A prepared passage can be useful practice, while transfer to different text gives additional evidence. Keep a short note of the recurring issue and the agreed exercise so the next session can review something specific. This course description does not promise an Ijazah award or a fixed certification route; ask the academy directly about any qualification you seek and the actual requirements of the proposed teacher and programme.''')],
'quranic-arabic': [
('Quranic Arabic for beginners: what the course should address', '''Explain whether your immediate need is reading Arabic script or understanding words and structures within Quranic text. A learner can recite without understanding much vocabulary, while another may know individual words but struggle with connected reading. These are different starting points. Ask the teacher how the proposed exercises connect with your present abilities and which reading support is needed. A Quranic Arabic course should have a clear purpose rather than being chosen simply because it appears more advanced than a foundation reading course.'''),
('Building vocabulary through context and guided examples', '''Learning an isolated translation can be a useful first step, but a word should also be encountered in an appropriate context. Ask what the example demonstrates and how a change in form or sentence structure affects the explanation. Keep unfamiliar points for the teacher rather than assuming a remembered gloss fits every occurrence. A modest set of carefully explained examples can be more useful than collecting a large vocabulary list without understanding how it supports the passage being studied.'''),
('Quranic Arabic, spoken Arabic and Tafseer', '''These subjects can overlap without having identical goals. If conversational Arabic is your aim, clarify that before selecting this course; a focus on Quranic vocabulary does not automatically establish a speaking curriculum. Tafseer adds guided explanation that cannot be replaced by translating individual words. Discuss how the subjects relate and which comes first for your current level. At review, ask what you can now recognise or explain in another suitable example, so progress is measured through understanding rather than the number of terms written in a notebook.''')],
}
AUDIENCE_EXTRA = {
'kids': [
('Online Quran lessons for children: a practical first-week routine', '''Start by agreeing a repeatable place and time rather than introducing a demanding study target immediately. Have the device, reading materials and a quiet space ready. For a younger child, clarify how an appropriate adult can remain involved and how the tutor will communicate practice instructions. Ask the child to explain the task in their own words afterwards. If the explanation is unclear, request clarification instead of making the exercise larger. The early routine should reveal whether the level, timing and teaching format are workable.'''),
('Quran classes for kids near me or online: comparing the options', '''If a local class is available, compare the actual teaching arrangements. Ask how much individual reading and feedback the child receives, what parent communication looks like and whether the journey affects the routine. Group contact may matter for one learner; direct one-to-one correction may matter more for another. A trial should help assess the online option on its own merits, without pretending a city listing is a physical classroom. If attention remains difficult, discuss timing, task size and format before assuming that greater pressure will improve learning.''')],
'adults': [
('Private Quran lessons for adults: explain the skill you need', '''An adult enquiry can begin in ordinary language: recognising Arabic letters, reading a new line, correcting a recurring sound or returning to study after years away. Describe what is comfortable and what requires help. The tutor can then choose a suitable sample instead of inferring a level from age or the course you selected on a website. If reading aloud feels embarrassing, explain that before the trial and ask for a manageable first exchange. A practical correction and a clear next step are more useful than an uncomfortable attempt to appear further advanced.'''),
('Learning Quran online around a working week', '''Choose a routine from the week you normally have, including work, care responsibilities and the time needed to settle into the lesson. Separate a preferred window from an appointment the academy has confirmed. Ask how a missed or changed session is handled before paying, particularly if your work shifts vary. Home practice can focus on a small exercise already demonstrated by the teacher. If the task repeatedly feels unmanageable, describe the obstacle and review the plan rather than letting uncertainty accumulate until you abandon the course.''')],
'beginners': [
('Beginner Quran reading: from recognition to an independent attempt', '''A helpful progression connects the visible letter with a sound, introduces the signs being taught and then places the skill inside an appropriate connected example. The teacher should select the sequence from the assessment. Ask how a learner's recognition will be checked when examples appear in a different order. Being able to follow a repeated model is a useful beginning, but independent application needs its own check. If the learner confuses two forms or sounds, a smaller focused exercise may be more effective than repeating a whole page without understanding the distinction.'''),
('What beginner progress can look like before fluent reading', '''Early progress may mean recognising a form with fewer prompts, producing a modelled sound more clearly or reading a short joined word accurately. Ask the tutor to show the improvement in a suitable example and explain the next task. Do not compare a beginner's page count with another learner who started with different skills. Adults and children can both need first-letter work, while a returning learner may retain several foundations. Review actual reading and comfort with the explanation before treating a completed book or named level as proof that the next stage is appropriate.''')],
'female-teachers': [
('Female Quran tutor requests: the details that help matching', '''State whether lessons are for a child or adult, the current reading level, the preferred teaching language and realistic UK-time windows. Explain that a female teacher is requested before assuming that a particular appointment is available. Ask who would teach the trial and regular lessons, and how any substitution would be discussed. Tutor preference matters, while course suitability still depends on actual reading and the quality of explanation. Confirm both parts of the arrangement instead of choosing solely from a generic academy description.'''),
('Assessing explanation, comfort and communication in the trial', '''Observe whether the tutor listens to the learner, identifies a manageable difficulty and demonstrates a correction that can be attempted again. Ask for clarification if the language or terminology is unclear. Adults can discuss privacy and communication preferences; parents can ask how they will remain involved in a child's lessons. Agree the platform and contact route directly, together with the relevant attendance and recording policies. No location page or tutor preference establishes those arrangements by itself, so request a clear explanation before committing to a paid schedule.''')],
}
GUIDE_EXTRA = {
'best-online-quran-classes-uk': [
('A practical online Quran class comparison checklist', '''Write down the same questions for each academy so the comparison is fair. Include individual reading time, the teacher's explanation, lesson length, monthly frequency, currency and the procedure for missed sessions. Ask whether the teacher who takes the trial is expected to continue. For a child, discuss parent involvement; for an adult, explain privacy or work constraints. A low monthly price is difficult to interpret without the lesson details, while an expensive plan is not automatically a better fit. Keep evidence from the actual conversation beside the promotional claims.'''),
('Comparing Quran class fees without mixing currencies', '''Quran Hub's published standard plans are USD 30 for eight or USD 40 for sixteen 30-minute lessons per month, while Tafseer has separate fees. These are not pound quotations. Ask for the current payment arrangement and any conversion or provider charges before comparing with another academy advertising GBP prices. Also check whether the comparison concerns one learner, a private appointment or a group. This prevents a headline number from hiding a different service, and keeps the decision tied to the plan you would actually use.'''),
('When a promising academy may still be the wrong fit', '''A qualified teacher and an attractive timetable do not remove every practical mismatch. The learner may struggle to hear a distinction over the connection, need another explanation language or find the proposed time exhausting. Raise the issue during the trial and ask whether it can be addressed. If the answer remains unclear, compare another suitable option rather than treating a promotional ranking as a reason to ignore the difficulty. The best choice is the arrangement that can support this learner consistently, with terms that the household understands.''')],
'first-online-quran-trial': [
('What to bring to your first Quran trial lesson', '''Have the most recent book or passage ready, along with any notes about recurring difficulty. Explain what the learner can do without help and what still requires a prompt. If there is no previous experience, say so; inventing a level makes the assessment less useful. Choose the same device and room you would normally use. Ask the teacher to select an appropriate sample rather than preparing only a memorised performance, because the aim is a suitable recommendation, not an impressive first result.'''),
('Questions to ask after the teacher hears your reading', '''Ask which skill should come first, why that skill matters and what a suitable practice task would look like. A good explanation connects the recommendation with something heard during the trial. If the tutor introduces an unfamiliar term, request an example and try it. Then distinguish the educational recommendation from the administrative arrangement: confirm lesson length, teaching language, preferred teacher, current fee and recurring UK-time window separately. A free trial can help with both conversations, but it does not automatically reserve a particular appointment.'''),
('Making a decision after the free trial', '''Review the learner's comfort, the usefulness of the correction and the practicality of the proposed routine. For a child, ask what they understood instead of judging only whether they stayed perfectly still. For an adult, consider whether basic questions could be asked openly. If something felt unsuitable, describe the issue directly and ask about an alternative. You can use the experience to compare formats without assuming that every online lesson will feel the same or that one difficult first exercise defines the learner's ability.''')],
'uk-quran-class-times-gmt-bst': [
('A clear message for booking Quran lessons in UK local time', '''Use a date, day and local time in the enquiry, and identify the time as the UK clock. For example, a preferred early-evening window is information for the academy to check, not a confirmed slot. Ask for the actual appointment in writing and keep that message accessible to whoever helps the learner join. If a calendar invitation displays another time, resolve the difference before the session. Comparing the next dated appointment is safer than relying only on an old abbreviation in a previous conversation.'''),
('School calendars and clock changes are different questions', '''England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland share the UK GMT/BST pattern, but schools do not all share identical term and holiday dates. Give the dates relevant to your household and confirm whether the regular lesson continues during a break. Do not infer attendance rules from the city page or another family's timetable. An overseas teacher's calendar may also follow different seasonal changes. Ask explicitly whether the agreed lesson stays fixed to UK local time when the clocks change, and check the next date on both sides.'''),
('Planning travel without missing the time-zone detail', '''Before travel, compare the local time at your destination with the agreed UK appointment. Also check whether you will have a quiet room, a readable page and dependable sound. Joining remotely from another country may be technically possible while still being impractical at the local hour. Discuss any requested change beforehand and ask who confirms it. Scheduling flexibility, notice periods and make-up lessons are separate terms; the ability to send an online message does not define the academy's policy for a missed appointment.''')],
'quran-practice-for-children': [
('A calm home-practice sequence after online Quran lessons', '''Prepare the demonstrated exercise, ask the child to explain the task and listen to a manageable attempt. Encourage them to stop and identify uncertainty instead of racing through an unfamiliar page. If the correction is clear, practise it accurately in the way the tutor advised. If it is not, keep a note for the next lesson. Finish with a clear stopping point so practice remains a defined task rather than an open-ended demand to continue until the parent is satisfied.'''),
('Helping a child when you cannot assess Arabic pronunciation', '''A parent does not need to pretend to be the recitation teacher to provide useful support. You can organise materials, protect a realistic time and notice when the child cannot follow the instruction. Ask the tutor for a plain-language description of the task and how uncertainty should be reported. Avoid introducing a different correction from an unrelated source, particularly when you are unsure whether it concerns the same sound or rule. Consistency of instruction gives the teacher a better basis for checking what happened between lessons.'''),
('Reviewing a practice routine that is not working', '''Describe the pattern to the tutor: when the difficulty starts, what the child attempts and how much prompting is needed. Change one manageable factor, such as timing or the size of the exercise, and review the result. If discomfort persists, discuss the explanation, level and teaching format rather than simply extending practice. A short task completed with understanding can be more useful than a longer session built around conflict. The next goal should come from observed learning, not from another child's reported progress.''')],
'hifz-revision-around-school': [
('Separate new memorisation, recent review and older recall', '''Ask the teacher to describe the purpose of each part of the plan in practical terms. New material requires accurate preparation, while recent and older passages need to remain accessible without increasing dependence on prompts. A named revision system is useful only when the learner understands the actual workload. Write down what to prepare for the next lesson and which uncertainty to bring back. Avoid allowing all available time to be absorbed by new work while older material disappears from the routine.'''),
('Responding to repeated Hifz mistakes', '''Note the passage and where the same issue returns, then let the tutor hear it. Explain whether the difficulty concerns recall, the transition between passages or a correction that remains unclear. Do not guess at the cause from the number of completed verses. The teacher may recommend smaller new work or a focused review before extending the target. An honest account of uncertainty supports a better adjustment than hiding weaker material behind a fluent performance of the most recently prepared section.'''),
('Adjusting Hifz targets during exams or changing work shifts', '''Plan from the time normally available and flag a known busy period early. Ask what should remain in review and what new material can temporarily wait. Confirm attendance arrangements separately if the regular appointment also needs to change. After the busy period, check retention before trying to recover an imagined backlog. A sustainable plan should make the next task understandable and achievable, rather than force the learner to compare ordinary school or working weeks with someone else's holiday-intensive memorisation schedule.''')],
'safe-online-quran-tutor-questions': [
('Checking Quran teacher qualifications and academy claims', '''Ask what a stated qualification refers to, who issued it and how it relates to the teaching proposed. A broad academy claim does not answer every question about an individual tutor's experience with the learner's age or starting level. Ask who will conduct the trial and whether a different teacher may take regular lessons. Where a background-check claim matters, request the actual explanation directly; this site does not establish a DBS status. Keep the discussion focused on verifiable information instead of assuming that a badge or reassuring phrase defines a complete safeguarding arrangement.'''),
('Agreeing platform, contact and recording arrangements', '''For a child, ask how an appropriate adult can remain involved, how class links are shared and which communication route should be used. Discuss private messaging and who receives progress information. If recordings are proposed, clarify purpose, access, storage and consent before assuming they are available or desirable. These questions should be answered for the actual teaching arrangement, rather than borrowed from another academy's policy. A recording or platform feature can support a process, but it does not replace clear responsibilities and an accessible way to raise concerns.'''),
('What to do when the arrangement feels unsuitable', '''Know the contact person and procedure for a concern before you need them. Explain practical or teaching difficulties promptly, including a mismatch in communication, timing or the learner's comfort. Ask what alternatives can be considered and confirm any revised arrangement in writing. For a serious or urgent safeguarding issue, use the appropriate local reporting or emergency service rather than waiting for an ordinary course enquiry response. A family's decision should remain informed by the child's wellbeing and the actual agreement, not by pressure to continue because a trial has already happened.''')],
}
NATION_EXTRA = {
'England': [('Online Quran classes for families across England', 'A practical enquiry begins with the learner rather than a nationwide assumption about school or work. Explain whether the main aim is first-letter reading, recitation correction, memorisation or understanding. Then give the time windows your household can realistically keep. For a child changing teachers, bring the previous book or exercise so the assessment can connect with earlier work. For an adult returning after a break, describe retained skills as well as uncertainty. A city page can organise that enquiry, while the actual recommendation should still come from listening to the learner.')],
'Scotland': [('Online Quran learning alongside school, university or work in Scotland', 'Use your actual timetable when describing availability. A pupil, university student and shift worker may need different routines even within the same city. Ask which practice task is realistic between appointments and how a change will be discussed before a known busy period. If the learner is returning to study, bring a familiar passage and let the teacher select an appropriate new example as well. That comparison helps establish a suitable starting point without assuming either that every earlier skill has disappeared or that familiar recitation proves independent reading.')],
'Wales': [('Quran lessons in Wales: reading level and teaching language', 'A learner who studies other subjects through Welsh may still prefer English, Arabic or Urdu for Quran lesson explanations, while another household may have different needs. State the preferred language and test the actual explanation during the trial. Separately describe reading ability and the educational goal. A comfortable conversation does not remove the need to assess recitation, and a suitable reading course does not establish that every requested language is available. Confirm the tutor, explanation language and regular appointment together before choosing a paid plan.')],
'Northern Ireland': [('Preparing an online Quran enquiry from Northern Ireland', 'Include your actual location, whether the learner is a child or adult and the reading skill that needs attention. The directory distinguishes Bangor in Northern Ireland from Bangor in Wales and combines Derry/Londonderry on one route to make navigation clearer. Those naming choices do not create separate course standards or local classrooms. Ask the tutor to hear a suitable sample, explain a first learning priority and identify an appropriate practice task. The learner can then consider course fit alongside the practical appointment, teaching language and published fee information.')],
}
UK_EXTRA = [
('Online Quran classes for kids in the UK: matching support to readiness', 'Children can begin from very different starting points, even at similar ages. One may recognise letters but need help joining them, while another may recall surahs without independently reading a new passage. Describe these abilities separately in the enquiry. During a trial, notice whether the explanation is clear and the child gets an opportunity to try again after a correction. Ask how a parent can support the routine and report uncertainty. The next task should be something the learner understands, rather than an ambitious quantity chosen before the teacher has heard them.'),
('Online Quran classes for adults and beginners: a private starting assessment', 'An adult learner may be starting Arabic for the first time, returning after a long break or seeking correction of a recurring recitation issue. Explain the need without choosing a level purely from the course title. A useful assessment can compare a familiar passage with an appropriate new example, or begin with letters where needed. Ask for the reason behind the recommended course and a clear practice task. This helps connect the educational choice with actual evidence while leaving room to discuss work, privacy and UK-time availability separately.'),
('Choosing the best online Quran classes for your household', 'Compare the real lesson rather than an unsupported ranking. Check how much individual reading takes place, how corrections are demonstrated and whether the learner understands the next step. Ask about teacher continuity, teaching language, communication and missed-session arrangements. Put fees on the same basis by checking currency, duration, monthly frequency and course. A convenient time and an attractive price matter, but they do not compensate for an explanation that the learner cannot use. A free trial provides a practical opportunity to test the fit before deciding whether to continue.'),
]
